UID:a4-residency-chengdu-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: A4 Residency Art Center: International Residency Fellowsh
 ip 2028 (Chengdu)
DESCRIPTION:The A4 Residency Art Center in Chengdu\, the residency platform
  of the A4 Art Museum in Luxelakes Eco-City\, opens its full-year 2028 rec
 ruitment. The interdisciplinary\, open and collaborative platform fosters 
 creative exchange among artists\, scientists\, writers and other professio
 nals\, with residents initiating participatory local activities that conne
 ct them with the city and community\; A4 also organises group activities\,
  sharing sessions\, media promotion and exhibition opportunities. CYCLES: 
 Spring 2028 (April to June) and Autumn 2028 (July to October)\, each appro
 ximately eight weeks. WHAT YOU GET (International Residency Fellowship\, f
 ully funded): private studio and accommodation\, round-trip economy airfar
 e or second-class train within a set budget\, a production grant of RMB 10
 \,000 per person or team for materials and production (excluding living ex
 penses)\, community engagement and cultural experiences\, media promotion 
 and networking. ELIGIBILITY: multidisciplinary creators worldwide\, includ
 ing visual and performing artists\, musicians\, filmmakers\, architects\, 
 curators\, designers and creatives with interdisciplinary backgrounds brid
 ging art\, business and culture. APPLICATION: free\; submit a photo\, port
 folio\, CV and project proposal in English and Chinese to a4residency@a4am
 .cn\, with the subject line specifying the programme tier and preferred cy
 cle. DEADLINE: 31 December 2026 for the 2028 full-year recruitment. A para
 llel self-funded light track exists\; this listing covers the funded fello
 wship only.\n\nOrganisation: A4 Residency Art Center (A4 Art Museum)\nLoca
 tion: Luxelakes Eco-City\, Tianfu New Area\, Chengdu\, China. Open worldwi
 de.\nAward: Fully funded fellowship: private studio and accommodation\, ro
 und-trip economy airfare or second-class train ticket within a set budget\
 , and a production grant of RMB 10\,000 per person/team for materials and 
 production (excluding living expenses). No application fee.\nDuration: App
 roximately 8 weeks\, in the Spring 2028 (April to June) or Autumn 2028 (Ju
 ly to October) cycle.\n\nMore: https://www.a4artmuseum.com/en/a4-residency
 artcenter/residing/latest-recruitment/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.a4artmuseum.com/en/a4-residencyartcenter/residing/latest-re
 cruitment/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Luxelakes Eco-City\, Tianfu New Area\, Chengdu\, China. Open world
 wide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:braunschweig-projects-curatorial-fellowship-2027-28@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261004
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261005
SUMMARY:Deadline: Braunschweig Projects 2027/28: Curatorial Fellowship at H
 BK Braunschweig
DESCRIPTION:The curatorial strand of the Braunschweig Projects fellowship p
 rogramme at Braunschweig University of Art (HBK Braunschweig)\, funded by 
 the State of Lower Saxony\, offers one curator a twelve-month fellowship b
 eginning 1 April 2027. The fellow develops a curatorial concept culminatin
 g in a final exhibition and presentation in January/February 2028\; projec
 ts should ideally connect to a museum\, collection or cultural institution
  in Lower Saxony. WHAT YOU GET: EUR 1\,600 per month for twelve months\, a
  rent-free studio apartment designed for one person (with a EUR 250 monthl
 y contribution towards incidental living costs charged to the fellow)\, a 
 subsidy of up to EUR 4\,000 to support exhibition activities and a EUR 2\,
 000 travel budget. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must have gained initial curato
 rial experience of at least three years\; no formal degree is specified. F
 ellows may not hold regular income from third parties or other state fundi
 ng during the fellowship\, and must spend at least eight months of the HBK
  lecture period on site in Braunschweig. Participation in the Welcome Week
 s (19 to 30 April 2027) and the final presentation is mandatory. APPLICATI
 ON: submit a project draft covering research\, preparation\, implementatio
 n\, budget and communication plans via the HBK application portal. DEADLIN
 E: 4 October 2026\, 23:59. The artistic fellowships strand (fine arts and 
 sound art) runs in parallel with the same deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Braun
 schweig University of Art (HBK Braunschweig)\, on behalf of the State of L
 ower Saxony\nLocation: Braunschweig\, Germany. Open worldwide\; at least e
 ight months of the HBK lecture period must be spent on site.\nAward: Stipe
 nd of EUR 1\,600 per month for 12 months (EUR 19\,200 total)\, plus a rent
 -free one-person studio apartment in Braunschweig (the fellow pays EUR 250
  per month towards incidental living costs)\, a subsidy of up to EUR 4\,00
 0 for exhibition activities and a EUR 2\,000 travel budget. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Twelve months from 1 April 2027\, with a final presentati
 on in January/February 2028.\n\nMore: https://www.hbk-bs.de/hochschule/sti
 pendienprogramme/braunschweig-projects-kuratorinnen-stipendium/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.hbk-bs.de/hochschule/stipendienprogramme/braunschweig-proje
 cts-kuratorinnen-stipendium/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Braunschweig\, Germany. Open worldwide\; at least eight months of 
 the HBK lecture period must be spent on site.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pedantic-arts-residency-and-research-grant-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pedantic Arts Residency and Research Grant 2027 (Pittsbur
 gh)
DESCRIPTION:Pedantic Arts Residency opens applications for its Summer 2027 
 and Fall 2027 programmes in Pittsburgh: two four-week residency cohorts an
 d two research grants. THE RESIDENCY houses three creatives at a time (a v
 isual artist\, an organizer and a writer) in a collective four-week progra
 mme that emphasizes exploration\, exchange and community connection over p
 roduction. Residents get free furnished housing in a shared apartment\, a 
 USD 2\,000 stipend to cover living expenses (half on arrival\, half on com
 pletion)\, a Pittsburgh-based guide working in their field\, tickets and i
 nstitutional passes\, and introductions across the city's creative communi
 ty. THE RESEARCH GRANT invites an individual visual artist\, curator or wr
 iter for a one- to two-week independent stay to conduct Pittsburgh-related
  research\, with a free private bedroom at the Pedantic Apartment\, a USD 
 500 or USD 1\,000 honorarium usable at the grantee's discretion\, and help
  establishing relevant connections. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or 
 older and not Pittsburgh residents at the time of application\; travel cos
 ts are the participant's own. Residency attendance and staying in the prov
 ided housing are required. APPLICATION: applications are free until 28 Sep
 tember 2026 at 23:59 EST\; a USD 20 fee applies from then until the final 
 deadline of 15 October 2026 at 23:59 EST.\n\nOrganisation: Pedantic Arts R
 esidency\nLocation: Pittsburgh\, Pennsylvania\, USA. Open to non-Pittsburg
 h residents aged 18 or older\; travel is self-funded.\nAward: Residency: f
 ree furnished housing in a shared apartment plus a USD 2\,000 living stipe
 nd (half on arrival\, half on completion). Research Grant: a free private 
 bedroom at the Pedantic Apartment plus a USD 500 or USD 1\,000 honorarium 
 at the grantee's discretion. Application free until 28 September 2026\, th
 en USD 20.\nDuration: Residency: four weeks\, Summer 2027 or Fall 2027 coh
 orts. Research Grant: one to two weeks.\n\nMore: https://pedanticarts.org/
 announcements/open-call-for-residents-and-grantees-submit-by-october-15th/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pedanticarts.org/announcements/open-call-for-residents-and-gran
 tees-submit-by-october-15th/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Pittsburgh\, Pennsylvania\, USA. Open to non-Pittsburgh residents 
 aged 18 or older\; travel is self-funded.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:scca-ljubljana-award-for-curatorial-practices-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261023
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261024
SUMMARY:Deadline: World of Art / SCCA-Ljubljana: Award for Curatorial Pract
 ices 2026
DESCRIPTION:To mark the 30th anniversary of its World of Art school for cur
 atorial practices and writing\, SCCA-Ljubljana establishes the Award for C
 uratorial Practices\, recognizing outstanding achievements in curating con
 temporary visual art. The award is organized in partnership with Moderna g
 alerija (MG+MSUM)\, Cukrarna/MGML and the Center for Contemporary Arts Cel
 je\, with support from the Slovenian Ministry of Culture. WHAT YOU GET: tw
 o tiers are awarded in 2026\, a Large Award of EUR 5\,000 gross for an est
 ablished practice (minimum 10 completed curatorial projects within the las
 t 15 years) and a Small Award of EUR 2\,500 gross for an emerging practice
  (minimum 3 completed projects within the last 5 years). ELIGIBILITY: cura
 tors working within Slovenia's cultural sector\, or Slovenian curators who
 se projects have been realized in international institutions. APPLICATION:
  submit a single PDF (max 5MB) containing a portfolio\, a curatorial state
 ment\, a biography and a statement on the relevance of the practice (each 
 up to one A4 page)\, plus consent\, via the Google Form linked from the ca
 ll\; materials in English\, filename formatted NAME_SURNAME_Award for Cura
 torial Practices_2026. DEADLINE: 23 October 2026\, with the ceremony in De
 cember 2026.\n\nOrganisation: World of Art School\, SCCA-Ljubljana\, with 
 Moderna galerija (MG+MSUM)\, Cukrarna/MGML and Center for Contemporary Art
 s Celje\nLocation: Ljubljana\, Slovenia. For curators working in Slovenia'
 s cultural sector\, or Slovenian curators realizing projects in internatio
 nal institutions.\nAward: Two cash awards: a Large Award of EUR 5\,000 gro
 ss (minimum 10 completed projects in the last 15 years) and a Small Award 
 of EUR 2\,500 gross (minimum 3 completed projects in the last 5 years). No
  application fee.\nDuration: One-time award\; ceremony in December 2026.\n
 \nMore: https://www.worldofart.org/en/award-for-curatorial-practices-2026-
 open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.worldofart.org/en/award-for-curatorial-practices-2026-open-
 call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ljubljana\, Slovenia. For curators working in Slovenia's cultural 
 sector\, or Slovenian curators realizing projects in international institu
 tions.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:asymmetry-assistant-curator-fellowship-nottingham-2027@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Deadline: Asymmetry Assistant Curator Fellowship 2027-2029 at Notti
 ngham Contemporary
DESCRIPTION:Asymmetry Art Foundation and Nottingham Contemporary offer a fu
 lly funded two-year Assistant Curator Fellowship embedded in the curatoria
 l team of one of Europe's leading contemporary art institutions. The fello
 wship is geared towards an early-career curator who\, to any extent\, iden
 tifies with Chinese or Sinophone cultures or backgrounds\, continuing Asym
 metry's programme of curatorial fellowships at UK institutions (previous p
 lacements include Chisenhale Gallery and Whitechapel Gallery). WHAT YOU DO
 : contribute to exhibitions\, artist commissions\, live programmes\, resea
 rch\, writing and institutional development\, working across all stages of
  projects while developing independent curatorial work and joining Asymmet
 ry's international network of scholars and practitioners. WHAT YOU GET: an
  annual salary of GBP 27\,556 plus pension and National Insurance contribu
 tions\, annual leave plus UK bank holidays\, visa and legal fee assistance
  where required\, and a travel and relocation allowance. ELIGIBILITY: earl
 y-career curators with roughly 1 to 3 years of experience\, an educational
  background in art history\, cultural studies or related fields\, familiar
 ity with contemporary artistic practices\, direct experience collaborating
  with artists\, and proficiency in English. Applications must be in Englis
 h\; accessibility support is available via fellowship@asymmetryart.org. AP
 PLICATION: via the Asymmetry application portal. DEADLINE: 25 October 2026
 . The fellowship runs 6 April 2027 to 5 April 2029.\n\nOrganisation: Asymm
 etry Art Foundation\, with Nottingham Contemporary\nLocation: Nottingham C
 ontemporary\, Nottingham\, UK. Open internationally to early-career curato
 rs who identify with Chinese or Sinophone cultures or backgrounds\; visa a
 nd relocation support provided.\nAward: Annual salary of GBP 27\,556 for t
 wo years\, plus pension and National Insurance contributions\, annual leav
 e in line with organisational policy plus UK bank holidays\, visa and lega
 l fee assistance where required\, and a travel and relocation allowance. N
 o application fee.\nDuration: Two years\, 6 April 2027 to 5 April 2029.\n\
 nMore: https://www.asymmetryart.org/fellowships/open-calls/2027-asymmetry-
 assistant-curator-fellowship-nottingham-contemporary-opencall?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.asymmetryart.org/fellowships/open-calls/2027-asymmetry-assi
 stant-curator-fellowship-nottingham-contemporary-opencall?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Nottingham Contemporary\, Nottingham\, UK. Open internationally to
  early-career curators who identify with Chinese or Sinophone cultures or 
 backgrounds\; visa and relocation support provided.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ernest-albert-invitational-reflective-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261014
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261015
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ernest Albert Invitational 2027: Reflective Residency at 
 Cas-co x Kunsthal Mechelen
DESCRIPTION:Cas-co and Kunsthal Mechelen invite Belgium-based emerging cura
 tors\, writers\, critics\, researchers\, art historians and research-based
  practitioners to apply for the Ernest Albert Invitational 2027\, a four-m
 onth Reflective Residency at Cas-co in Leuven within the Off the Grid prog
 ramme. The Reflective Resident joins the four artists of Cas-co's Artistic
  Development Residency from 14 January to 14 May 2027\, developing an inde
 pendent research trajectory while closely following the questions\, proces
 ses and conversations emerging within the group. Reflection is understood 
 as an active practice: creating connections\, introducing perspectives\, a
 sking questions and generating new contexts around artistic work. The resi
 dency culminates in a Reflective contribution to the public programme of t
 he group exhibition at Kunsthal Mechelen\, 4 June to 29 August 2027\, cura
 ted by Jessica Meuleman (Kunsthal Mechelen)\, Zuzanna Rachowska and Arthur
  Cordier (Cas-co). SUPPORT: EUR 1\,100 artist fee per month\, EUR 120 tran
 sportation allowance per month\, and a one-time production budget of EUR 6
 00 (EUR 5\,480 total including VAT). ELIGIBILITY: Belgium-based\; priority
  goes to applicants early in their careers\, with limited institutional ac
 cess\, or developing interdisciplinary research practices around contempor
 ary art. HOW TO APPLY: one PDF (max 10 MB) with a description of your prac
 tice\, a proposal\, CV and portfolio\, via the online application form (Go
 ogle Form linked from the call page). Jury meets 26 October 2026\; selecti
 on announced 4 November 2026. Queries to arthur[at]cas-co.be. Deadline 14 
 October 2026 at 23:59 CET.\n\nOrganisation: Cas-co\, Leuven and Kunsthal M
 echelen\nLocation: Cas-co\, Leuven\, Belgium\, within the Off the Grid pro
 gramme\, with the closing exhibition at Kunsthal Mechelen. Open only to Be
 lgium-based applicants.\nAward: EUR 5\,480 total including VAT: an artist 
 fee of EUR 1\,100 per month\, a transportation allowance of EUR 120 per mo
 nth\, and a one-time production budget of EUR 600. No application fee.\nDu
 ration: Four months\, 14 January to 14 May 2027\, culminating in a contrib
 ution to the group exhibition's public programme at Kunsthal Mechelen from
  4 June to 29 August 2027\n\nMore: https://cas-co.be/nieuws/cas-coxkunstha
 lmechelen-artist-open-call-deadline-28-10-26-2?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cas-co.be/nieuws/cas-coxkunsthalmechelen-artist-open-call-deadl
 ine-28-10-26-2?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cas-co\, Leuven\, Belgium\, within the Off the Grid programme\, wi
 th the closing exhibition at Kunsthal Mechelen. Open only to Belgium-based
  applicants.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nars-foundation-curatorial-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Deadline: NARS Foundation: Curatorial Fellowship 2027 (NYC)
DESCRIPTION:NARS Foundation in Sunset Park\, Brooklyn\, invites application
 s for its Curatorial Fellowship\, a two-season fellowship running January 
 to June 2027 alongside the international and US artist residency programs.
  The fellow works closely with the artists-in-residence and NARS staff to 
 develop the season group exhibitions: conducting studio visits\, developin
 g curatorial plans and frameworks for the shows\, and writing the press re
 lease for each exhibition\, with an on-site presence of at least two days 
 a week. The fellowship carries a USD 3\,000 stipend for the six months. EL
 IGIBILITY: curators of any age\, gender and nationality who are currently 
 based in New York City and have lived in NYC for at least three years\; ap
 plicants may not be enrolled in a college or university art degree-grantin
 g program during the fellowship period. Decisions are communicated three t
 o four weeks after submission. Deadline 21 September 2026 at 11:59 PM ET. 
 Apply via Submittable at https://narsfoundation.submittable.com/submit/366
 631/.\n\nOrganisation: NARS Foundation\nLocation: Brooklyn\, New York\, US
  (201 46th Street\, Sunset Park). Open only to curators based in New York 
 City for at least three years\; on-site presence at least two days a week.
 \nAward: USD 3\,000 stipend for the six-month fellowship\, plus sustained 
 access to the NARS international and US artist residency community and sta
 ff support for the resulting exhibitions. No application fee listed for th
 e curatorial track.\nDuration: Six months\, January to June 2027\, spannin
 g two residency seasons\n\nMore: https://www.narsfoundation.org/residencie
 s/apply?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.narsfoundation.org/residencies/apply?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brooklyn\, New York\, US (201 46th Street\, Sunset Park). Open onl
 y to curators based in New York City for at least three years\; on-site pr
 esence at least two days a week.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucla-meap-modern-endangered-archives-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261117
SUMMARY:Deadline: UCLA Modern Endangered Archives Program (MEAP): 2026-27 C
 all for Applications
DESCRIPTION:The UCLA Library Modern Endangered Archives Program (MEAP)\, fu
 nded by Arcadia\, grants its ninth cohort of awards to preserve\, document
  and digitize cultural heritage collections at risk from environmental con
 ditions\, political uncertainty\, inherently unsustainable media\, inappro
 priate storage\, or communal and social change. IN SCOPE: 20th and 21st ce
 ntury materials\, including print\, photographic\, film\, video\, audio\, 
 ephemeral and born-digital objects\; all funded materials are published op
 en access in the UCLA Library Digital Collections repository. GRANT TYPES:
  Planning Grants up to USD 20\,000 over one year for organization\, survey
  and inventory work before digitization\; Project Grants up to USD 70\,000
  over two years for digitization and metadata creation on collections read
 y to digitize\, or for curating existing digital assets\; Regional Grants 
 up to USD 100\,000 over two years for digital collections combining materi
 als from three or more institutions\, families or archival repositories ar
 ound one theme\, community or historical event\, hosted by an organizing i
 nstitution with prior MEAP grant experience (consult MEAP staff before app
 lying). There is also an invitation-only Emergency Grant track up to USD 1
 0\,000 (email meap@library.ucla.edu)\, and a new AV Grants track up to USD
  100\,000 for audiovisual archives opening April 2027 with selections in S
 eptember 2027. TIMELINE: applications opened 14 September 2026\; prelimina
 ry application webinar 15 October 2026\; preliminary applications due Mond
 ay 16 November 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific Time via meap.smapply.io\; prelimin
 ary decisions December 2026\; detailed applications due 12 February 2027\;
  awards announced summer 2027. Applications name teams working with the in
 stitutions\, families or repositories that hold the collections\; read the
  Program Guidelines and Project Eligibility documents on the MEAP site bef
 ore applying.\n\nOrganisation: UCLA Library\, Modern Endangered Archives P
 rogram\nLocation: Worldwide. Projects digitize at-risk collections where t
 hey are held\; digitized materials are published open access in the UCLA L
 ibrary Digital Collections.\nAward: Planning Grants up to USD 20\,000 (up 
 to 1 year) for collection evaluation\, survey and inventory work before di
 gitization. Project Grants up to USD 70\,000 (up to 2 years) for digitizat
 ion and metadata creation on well-organized collections\, or curation of e
 xisting digital assets. Regional Grants up to USD 100\,000 (up to 2 years)
  for digital collections drawing on three or more institutions\, families 
 or repositories around one theme\, hosted by an organization with prior ME
 AP experience. A separate AV Grants track (up to USD 100\,000\, for audio\
 , video and film) opens in April 2027. No application fee.\nDuration: Plan
 ning Grants up to 1 year\; Project and Regional Grants up to 2 years\n\nMo
 re: https://meap.library.ucla.edu/call-for-applications/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://meap.library.ucla.edu/call-for-applications/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide. Projects digitize at-risk collections where they are he
 ld\; digitized materials are published open access in the UCLA Library Dig
 ital Collections.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tpg-future-of-archives-whose-archive-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Photographers' Gallery: Future of Archives\, Whose Ar
 chive is it Anyway? (Call for Speakers)
DESCRIPTION:Whose Archive is it Anyway? is the first in a three-part series
  of events at The Photographers' Gallery exploring the future of archives 
 and archiving. This one-day event on Friday 16 April 2027 kickstarts the c
 onversation with commissioned talks addressing what it means to make an ar
 chive today\, who decides what an archive consists of\, who creates an arc
 hive\, who an archive is for\, and who has access. The gallery welcomes su
 bmissions of real-life experiences rather than academic papers that challe
 nge or answer these or other points related to the future of archives. SUG
 GESTED TOPICS: artist and photographers' legacies\; institutional archives
 \; memory (forgotten or lost histories made visible again)\; truth within 
 archives (who is telling the stories and how)\; ownership (who has the aut
 hority to tell stories\, who owns the images and their stories)\; relation
 ships between those who create archives and those who use them\; radical\,
  non-traditional archives that redesign how stories are gathered and held 
 to safeguard living memories\; family archives\; reclaimed photographic ar
 chives\; counter archives that question dominant historical narratives\; g
 rassroots archives\; technologies that reduce barriers to archives while s
 afeguarding material\; digital preservation and digitisation\; and dialogi
 c archives. ELIGIBILITY: open to all\, especially those with experience of
  working with archives and the stories within them\; applicants must be 18
  or older and available to attend The Photographers' Gallery in London on 
 16 April 2027 to speak at the event. Selected speakers receive a fee of GB
 P 200 and will be notified by 18 January 2027. Questions to archive@tpg.or
 g.uk. Deadline 1 November 2026\, 11:59pm GMT.\n\nOrganisation: The Photogr
 aphers' Gallery\nLocation: London\, UK. Selected speakers must be availabl
 e to speak in person at The Photographers' Gallery on 16 April 2027.\nAwar
