UID:stroom-den-haag-pro-art-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stroom Den Haag: PRO Art Program
DESCRIPTION:Stroom Den Haag's PRO Art Program scheme contributes to the cos
 ts of an art programme: a series of activities (exhibitions\, presentation
 s\, lectures\, workshops\, etc.) with an artistic\, substantive\, thematic
  and/or formal connection that take place within a certain period. It also
  covers exchange activities with other countries and programmes for guest 
 studios and artist-in-residencies. WHO CAN APPLY: organisations that reali
 se the programme on a non-profit basis\, including artist initiatives\, co
 llectives and associations\, plus independent curators and organisers. App
 licants do not have to be based in The Hague: Hague artists apply via thei
 r Stroom registration (Mijn Stroom)\, outsiders apply with a CV\, and all 
 participants must provide CVs or current Stroom registration. ASSESSMENT: 
 intrinsic quality and coherence\, added value for The Hague's art climate\
 , potential to catalyse broader activity\, budget proportionality\, audien
 ce engagement\, and fair artist pay. APPLICATION: via the Stroom portal wi
 th an 80-word summary\, a plan of max 2\,500 words\, a budget using Stroom
 's model\, 10 to 20 images with a documentation list\, and optionally up t
 o 9 minutes of video. NOT ELIGIBLE: applications connected to formal (art)
  education programmes or from current students. DEADLINE: the last 2026 ro
 und closes 25 October 2026. The grant is valid for up to two years after t
 he decision.\n\nOrganisation: Stroom Den Haag\nLocation: The Hague\, Nethe
 rlands. Aimed at non-profit organisations\, artist initiatives\, collectiv
 es and independent curators/organisers\; applicants from outside The Hague
  apply with a CV\, provided the programme benefits The Hague's art climate
 .\nAward: Maximum EUR 10\,000 per application\, up to EUR 20\,000 per appl
 icant per calendar year. Requests above EUR 5\,000 require at least 30 per
 cent co-financing of the total budget from third parties. Compliance with 
 the kunstenaarshonorarium.nl artist fee guideline is part of the assessmen
 t. No application fee.\nDuration: Project-based\; the grant remains valid 
 for up to two years after the decision meeting\, with 80 percent paid upfr
 ont and 20 percent on the final report\n\nMore: https://www.stroom.nl/en/s
 troom-for-artists/financial-support/pro-art-program?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.stroom.nl/en/stroom-for-artists/financial-support/pro-art-p
 rogram?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Hague\, Netherlands. Aimed at non-profit organisations\, artis
 t initiatives\, collectives and independent curators/organisers\; applican
 ts from outside The Hague apply with a CV\, provided the programme benefit
 s The Hague's art climate.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:brooks-international-fellowship-tate-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260925T223601Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Brooks International Fellowship Programme 2027 (Tate Brit
 ain x Delfina Foundation)
DESCRIPTION:Tate\, in collaboration with Delfina Foundation\, invites appli
 cations for the thirteenth Brooks International Fellowship Programme: two 
 6-month fellowships in 2027 for international researchers based outside th
 e UK\, hosted in the Tate Britain Curatorial Division and complemented by 
 activities and residency life at Delfina Foundation. FELLOWSHIP 1\, Leonor
 a Carrington: Art and the Theatre of Life: foundational research into the 
 British-Mexican artist's interest in theatre\, performance and performativ
 ity\, locating\, analysing and contextualising theatrical materials across
  archives\, manuscripts\, correspondence\, photographs and ephemera\, and 
 situating them within Carrington's transnational career\; a Fellow with kn
 owledge of the Mexican social-political context\, culture and performance 
 histories of the second half of the twentieth century is particularly well
  placed. FELLOWSHIP 2\, Artist Supported Studios: contributing to research
  and development of the 2028 Tate Britain Commission through the lens of a
 rtists who work with supported studios (organisations enabling disabled an
 d neurodivergent artists to develop sustained professional practices)\, ex
 ploring how galleries\, commissioned artists and supported studios can dev
 elop different forms of communication and ways of working through the comm
 issioning process\, how collaborative practices can be documented and inte
 rpreted while remaining attentive to artistic agency\, and how the researc
 h can shape future institutional practice. Fellows live at Delfina Foundat
 ion with a desk in their room plus hot-desking\, work at Tate Britain\, co
 llaborate across Tate sites\, get access to Tate's collection\, archive\, 
 staff and networks\, and present their practice at Tate and Delfina events
 . APPLICATION: download and return the Word application form from the Tate
  jobs page (only returned Word forms are accepted\; nothing is filled in o
 nline). DEADLINE: Wednesday 21 October 2026\, 17:00 GMT.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Tate\, in collaboration with Delfina Foundation\nLocation: London\, UK (wo
 rking at Tate Britain\, living at Delfina Foundation). Open to internation
 al researchers based outside the UK.\nAward: Fully funded 6-month fellowsh
 ip: living allowance bursary of GBP 40 per day\, single bedroom at Delfina
  Foundation (house of up to 8 residents with shared facilities)\, return e
 conomy flight\, UK airport transfers\, visa fee reimbursement with visa su
 pport\, London Underground and bus travel pass\, a research resource allow
 ance\, and travel to other Tate galleries (Tate Modern\, Liverpool\, St Iv
 es) and other UK and European cultural institutions. Two fellowships avail
 able. No application fee.\nDuration: Six months\, 5 April to 17 September 
 2027\n\nMore: https://jobsearch.tate.org.uk/jobs/job/Brooks-International-
 Fellow-Programme/839?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://jobsearch.tate.org.uk/jobs/job/Brooks-International-Fellow-Prog
 ramme/839?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK (working at Tate Britain\, living at Delfina Foundatio
 n). Open to international researchers based outside the UK.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
